Found by the format lane while measuring karr #102 (sops 3.13.3 at /tmp/sops).
Pre-existing. A type divergence only -- the MAC holds and both implementations
read the file, so nothing fails and nothing warns.

  plaintext:  v: 1e-400          (also 1e-500, 0.<399 zeros>1)

  sops -e     encrypted slot -> type:float, unencrypted slot rewritten to `0`
              sops -d exit 0, reads the float 0
  File::SOPS  YAML::XS returns IOK+NOK+POK with NV 0, detect_type says INT,
              encrypted slot -> type:int, unencrypted slot written as `1e-400`
              verbatim, sops -d exit 0 (measured)

Both sides digest the bytes `0` -- ours from the int rung, Go's from
strconv.FormatFloat(0,'f',-1,64) -- so the MAC agrees by construction and the
document is valid either way. What differs is the type sops hands the value on
as, exactly the class ADR 0019 / karr #92 warns about for a string Go reads as a
boolean. Nothing warns here: _go_retypes only checks %GO_BOOL_TOKEN.

Why it is a separate thing from #102, and why #102's fix cannot catch it: Go's
decimal.floatBits raises its overflow flag on OVERFLOW only, so ParseFloat
underflow returns 0 with no error and yaml.v3 keeps the float. Measured at the
bit: 2**1024-2**970 is where the overflow behaviour flips, and there is no
matching flip at the small end -- 1e-400 and 1e-500 are floats to Go and finite
zeros to Perl. #102's predicate is "our NV is non-finite", which cannot fire here.

Two questions, neither answered: whether _go_retypes should widen from the
boolean tokens to any leaf whose Go-resolved TYPE differs while its bytes agree
(that is a general widening, and it would need its own corpus), and whether an
underflowing literal is worth a rung of its own in detect_type. Low because a
config file holding 1e-400 is close to hypothetical.

Wire lane. Needs the sops binary to prove.
Note on the mechanism: the overflow/underflow ASYMMETRY is measured here (overflow -> str, underflow -> float 0, both against sops 3.13.3). The explanation offered above -- that Go's decimal.floatBits raises its range flag on overflow only, so ParseFloat underflow returns 0 with no error -- is read from Go's source from memory and was NOT verified in this run. Confirm it against strconv/decimal.go before relying on it.
